{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788186159,"nonce":"Ax1J9mI7q2Lv+d8Dm5Xa8C+so2k=","message":"RESOLVED DESIGN, from Evan's clarification 2026-08-31, and scoped for immediate\nbuild. His words: \"all we need for combat is to flip that thing on and to\nconfigure it in a way that respects the pawns skills, or to make it not on at\nall, they also always need to make sure flee mode is set to attack, not flee or\nignore. both need to be controls.\"\n\nTWO CONTROLS. ONE OF THEM ALREADY EXISTS.\n\n**Control B, hostility response, IS SHIPPED.** 3.4's `assign {hostility:\"Attack\"}`\nsets `playerSettings.hostilityResponse`, gated on `RaceProps.Humanlike` per\nPawnColumnWorker_HostilityResponse, and it already refuses `Attack` for a\nViolent-disabled pawn because HostilityResponseModeUtility's own menu omits it\nthere. No new setter — one lever, one owner. What was missing is that nothing\nmade the pair VISIBLE together, which is how it silently drifts.\n\n**Control A, `seek-at-will`, is the new verb.**\n\n  seek-at-will {pawns, on?, stance?, dry_run?}\n\n- `pawns` — the standard plural resolver (ids, or \"colonists\").\n- `on` — true | false | \"auto\".\n- `stance` — \"close\" | \"medium\" | \"far\" | \"auto\".\n- **Neither `on` nor `stance` present = a pure READ.** Report current state and\n  mutate nothing, so the agent can check without toggling.\n\nGATE — `Patch_PawnGetGizmos.ShowsSeekGizmo`, clause for clause, each refusal\nnaming its own gate:\n\n  pawn.Faction == Faction.OfPlayer          -\u003e gate \"not-player-faction\"\n  !pawn.RaceProps.Animal                    -\u003e gate \"animal\"\n  !pawn.Drafted                             -\u003e gate \"drafted\"\n  !pawn.WorkTagIsDisabled(WorkTags.Violent) -\u003e gate \"violent-disabled\"\n\nThe `!Drafted` clause is the whole point of the feature and must not be softened:\nSeek at Will is what an UNDRAFTED pawn does. Drafting is the micromanagement\nthis replaces.\n\nMOD INTEROP — reflection only, no hard reference. SeekAndKill is not a\ndependency; it merely happens to be on the bench. Types resolved once and\ncached. When it is not loaded the verb answers `ok:false` with code\n`mod-not-loaded` and a detail naming the mod, rather than throwing. Template is\nSeekAndKill's own PSInterop, which reflects into Perspective Shift and goes\ninert rather than guessing.\n\nTOGGLE PATH — `MpCompat.SyncedToggleSeek(pawn)`, NOT `SeekRegistry.Toggle`. The\ngizmo's own comment says why: the seek set is saved state the think tree reads\non every client. And because it is a FLIP, read `IsToggled` first and only call\nwhen current != requested; an already-correct pawn is an `already` rejection,\nmatching 3.4's convention rather than double-toggling back to wrong.\n\nSTANCE IS A RANGE FRACTION, which is what makes \"respects the pawns skills\"\nconcrete. From SeekStanceExtensions: Close = 0.35 of weapon range, Far = 0.9,\nMedium = the mod's tuned setting.\n\n`stance:\"auto\"`, and the rule is echoed in the result so it is auditable:\n\n  melee weapon, or no weapon        -\u003e Close   (must close to fight at all)\n  Brawler trait                     -\u003e Close\n  ranged weapon, Shooting \u003e= 8      -\u003e Far     (use the reach, stay safe)\n  ranged weapon, Shooting \u003c= 4      -\u003e Close   (hit chance rises sharply as\n                                                range closes, so a poor shot\n                                                should close, not hang back)\n  otherwise                         -\u003e Medium\n\n`on:\"auto\"` — \"or to make it not on at all\":\n\n  ON  if the pawn has a ranged weapon, or Melee \u003e= 6\n  OFF otherwise (an unarmed, unskilled pawn set to seek is a casualty)\n\nEvery pawn's decision reports the reason, so \"why is this one off\" is answerable\nwithout re-deriving the rule.\n\nTHE PAIRING WARNING — this is how \"they always need to make sure\" gets enforced\nwithout duplicating the setter. `seek-at-will` reports each pawn's\n`hostility_response`, and emits a WARNING for any pawn that is seeking while its\nhostility is not `Attack`. The agent cannot end up with a seeking pawn set to\nFlee and not know.\n\nREAD SIDE — `pawn`'s `state` section gains `seek: {toggled, stance}`, null when\nthe mod is absent.\n\nACCEPTANCE, verified by the orchestrator on the bench\n\n1. Gates: a DRAFTED pawn is refused with gate `drafted`; an animal with `animal`.\n2. Toggle two armed colonists on with `stance:\"auto\"`; read back `IsToggled`\n   true and stances matching the stated rule for their weapons and skills.\n3. `assign {hostility:\"Attack\"}` on the same pawns, then a `seek-at-will` READ\n   shows no warning.\n4. Set one pawn's hostility to `Flee`; the warning appears and names the pawn.\n5. **THE BULLET:** `dev:incident` a raid, advance, and show the seeking pawns\n   engaging — with NO `draft` and NO `attack` row anywhere in the journal for\n   the engagement. The only `action` rows are the toggles. That is the whole\n   claim: combat without micromanagement.\n6. Zero red errors, and no force-pausing modal left behind.","files":null}]}